{"name":"Ruffled Parasol","hex":"#fbc0b4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ruffled","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of ruffle"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having ruffles.","Puffed up like a bird's feathers."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ruffled"},{"term":"parasol","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small light umbrella used as protection from the sun.","A miniature paper umbrella used as a decoration in tropical-themed cocktails."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To protect with, or as if with, a parasol; to shade."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/parasol"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ruffle (sewing)","blocks":[{"text":"In sewing and dressmaking, a ruffle, frill, or furbelow is a strip of fabric, lace or ribbon tightly gathered or pleated on one edge and applied to a garment, bedding, or other textile as a form of trimming."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Gullager_Salisbury.jpg/330px-Gullager_Salisbury.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ruffle_(sewing)","wikidata":"Q2204140"},{"title":"Umbrella","blocks":[{"text":"An umbrella is a folding canopy supported by wooden or metal ribs that is mounted on a wooden, metal, or plastic pole. It is usually designed to protect a person against sun or rain. Initially, they were used in warmer countries for shade from the sun, but later evolved to also be used for protection from rain. Etymologically, the term umbrella is to be used when protecting from the sun, but is also commonly used when protecting from rain. Some countries specifically use the words parasol and parapluie to differentiate based on their use. There are also combinations of parasol and parapluie that are called en-tout-cas. A modern hand-held umbrella or parasol may have a black exterior canopy and a silver inner coating, for better protection from both the sun and ultraviolet rays, and may be water-resistant."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/M0354_000727-005_1.jpg/330px-M0354_000727-005_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Umbrella","wikidata":"Q41607"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Singer_Ruffler_Attachment.jpg/500px-Singer_Ruffler_Attachment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Singer_Ruffler_Attachment.jpg/1280px-Singer_Ruffler_Attachment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Singer_Ruffler_Attachment.jpg/500px-Singer_Ruffler_Attachment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mechanical ruffler by Singer, used on domestic sewing machines","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASinger_Ruffler_Attachment.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Flounce_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Flounce_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Flounce_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Flounce_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sketch of a flounce","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFlounce_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Zenkoji-Nagano.JPG/500px-Zenkoji-Nagano.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Zenkoji-Nagano.JPG/1280px-Zenkoji-Nagano.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wiki